  "awb---faq-data": "<div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><script> window.awbFAQ = {\"heading\": \"Church Survey Questions, Answered\", \"items\": [{\"q\": \"What is a church survey?\", \"a\": \"A church survey is a short questionnaire a church sends to its members or its guests to learn what they need and how its ministries are working. The largest is usually an annual survey of the whole congregation. Smaller church surveys follow a season of groups or a first visit.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do you create a church survey?\", \"a\": \"List what the church wants to decide, draft questions for each ministry involved, and test them on a few members first. Emergent's <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-survey-and-quiz-builder'>AI survey builder</a> turns that description into a working survey and a results page by ministry.\"}, {\"q\": \"What are some good questions to ask in a church survey?\", \"a\": \"Good church survey questions ask about things the church can change. Ask how welcome people felt on their first visit, which service they attend, whether they know how to join a group, and how they prefer to hear news. End with one open question, such as what the church should start doing.\"}, {\"q\": \"How many church survey questions should you ask?\", \"a\": \"About 15 is plenty for an annual congregation survey, and 4 is enough for a guest card. People drop out of long surveys before the last page. Cut repeated rating questions before you cut the open question at the end.\"}, {\"q\": \"What are some church survey examples?\", \"a\": \"Next to the annual congregation survey, churches run short surveys with one purpose. Common ones are a check-in with parents after a term of kids' programs or a question for people who stopped attending. Paid staff need their own <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-survey-and-quiz-builder/employee-engagement'>employee engagement survey</a>, since their answers concern their jobs.\"}, {\"q\": \"What should a church survey template include?\", \"a\": \"A useful church survey template opens with who will read the answers and a promise that no names are collected. Then come attendance and age band, the core questions you repeat every year, one section per ministry, and an open question. Emergent builds that structure from a plain description, and any question can be changed with a prompt.\"}, {\"q\": \"Should a church survey collect names?\", \"a\": \"Not for the annual survey. Promise anonymity on the first screen, drop the name and email fields, and never report on a group smaller than 5. Guest cards are the exception: a visitor who wants a call has to leave a phone number, and the card should say who will call.\"}, {\"q\": \"How often should churches send surveys?\", \"a\": \"Once a year works for the full congregation survey. Run it in the same month each time and the numbers compare cleanly. Shorter ones fit around a moment, like the end of a groups season or a decision on service times. In an Emergent-built survey, every year's answers stay in a database you own.\"}, {\"q\": \"How do you collect more church survey responses?\", \"a\": \"Give people a few minutes during the service, with the QR code on screen and paper cards in the seats. Open by naming one thing that changed because of last year's answers. Emergent-built surveys have no response cap, even for a congregation of several thousand.\"}, {\"q\": \"How should a church share survey results with members?\", \"a\": \"Share a short summary within a month: how many answered, the main findings, and what the leadership team will do about each. Leave out anything that could point to a small group. For one-off comments after an event, a simple <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-form-builder/feedback'>feedback form</a> is lighter than another survey.\"}]}; </script></div></div>",
